@page "/getting-started"
@attribute [Soenneker.Razor.Sitemap.Sitemap(Url = "https://flywheel.soenneker.com/getting-started/")]
<FlywheelSeoHead Title="Get Started with .NET Background Jobs | Flywheel" Description="Install Flywheel, configure Redis storage, and run your first .NET background job with source-generated dispatch. Follow the complete C# setup example." />
<article class="docs">
<nav class="docs-nav" aria-label="Guides"><Anchor Href="/getting-started/" aria-current="page">Runner setup</Anchor><Anchor Href="/dashboard/">Dashboard setup</Anchor></nav>
<H1>Run your first job.</H1>
<Paragraph>Flywheel supports several storage mechanisms. This guide uses Redis as one option. To follow this example, use .NET 10 and Redis 6.0.9 or newer.</Paragraph>
<H2>1. Create an app</H2>
<CodeExample Text="@CreateAppExample" Label="Create an app commands" Language="shell" />
<Paragraph>Add <code>PrivateAssets="all"</code> to the Generators package reference in your project file. Start Redis at <code>localhost:6379</code>, or update the connection string below.</Paragraph>
<H2>2. Define and enqueue a job</H2>
<Paragraph>Replace <code>Program.cs</code> with:</Paragraph>
<CodeExample Text="@FirstJobExample" Label="Complete first job example" Language="csharp" />
<Paragraph>The source generator creates <code>FlywheelJobs</code> and typed invokers at build time. Dispatch calls your job method directly, without runtime reflection.</Paragraph>
<H2>3. Run it</H2>
<CodeExample Text="@RunExample" Label="Run the application command" Language="shell" />
<Paragraph>Look for “Hello from Flywheel” in the console. Enqueue saves the job to your configured storage; a background worker executes it. In your application services, inject <code>IJobClient</code> to submit work.</Paragraph>
<Paragraph>Jobs can run more than once. Make handlers safe to retry and pass cancellation tokens through to async work.</Paragraph>
<H2 id="worker-pools">Worker pools</H2>
<Paragraph>Reserve workers for specific workloads. Named pools share the server's worker budget; default receives the remainder.</Paragraph>
<CodeExample Text="@PoolsExample" Label="Worker pool configuration" Language="csharp" />
<Paragraph>This leaves three workers in default. Route a job method to a pool with:</Paragraph>
<CodeExample Text="@PoolJobExample" Label="Job pool routing" Language="csharp" />
<Paragraph>Methods without a pool list use default. Each pool needs at least one worker at startup. Servers shows each pool's capacity and active jobs. See the <Anchor Href="https://github.com/soenneker/soenneker.flywheel.suite/blob/main/docs/core.md#worker-pools">pool reference</Anchor> for resizing and limits.</Paragraph>
<H2>Queue controls</H2>
<Paragraph>Use the dashboard's … menu to move queued work to the top or bottom, or release a delayed execution with Run now. The same controls are available through IJobClient:</Paragraph>
<CodeExample Text="@QueueExample" Label="Move an execution to the top of the queue" Language="csharp" />
<Paragraph>Other actions are <code>MoveToBottom</code> and <code>RunNow</code>. Moves affect the next attempt and respect pool, concurrency, and rate limits. See the <Anchor Href="https://github.com/soenneker/soenneker.flywheel.suite/blob/main/docs/core.md#queue-controls">queue API reference</Anchor>.</Paragraph>
<H2>Next steps</H2>
<Paragraph><Anchor Href="/dashboard/">Add the dashboard</Anchor> to search jobs and inspect executions, or see <Anchor Href="https://github.com/soenneker/soenneker.flywheel.suite/blob/main/docs/core.md#queue-and-schedule-jobs">scheduling and recurring jobs</Anchor>.</Paragraph>
</article>

